Title: ZODIAC WARS: THE RISE OF ARIES
Credit: Draft 1 — PROPOSED, not approved
Notes: ACT TWO of three. Continues from ACT_1.fountain.

FADE IN:

EXT. MERIDIAN SPACEPORT, EARTH — DAWN

A gleaming, ordinary spaceport: arrivals halls of pale stone, planetary flags of the twelve Houses limp on tall masts. Kael's battered hauler touches down between two glossy diplomatic shuttles and looks like a dented kettle between teacups.

Court OFFICERS in grey sashes are waiting on the apron. At their head, Adaeze, with a notebook under her arm. She does not look like someone who has slept.

The hauler ramp lowers. Kael descends, soot still on his face, red mark glowing on his forearm. He is exhausted and rigid with it.

ADAEZE
You're Kael Marren.

KAEL
How do you know that?

ADAEZE
A petition was filed with the Court at midnight. It names you as a fugitive from the Ares Foundry, wanted for the murder of its Master.

Kael stares at her.

KAEL
I didn't — She was my —

ADAEZE
I know what the petition says. I am not asking whether it is true. I am telling you that under Clause four, an accused person arriving from a House that has filed a complaint must be held until the complaint is heard.

KAEL
Held.

ADAEZE
In comfort. With food. And a lawyer, if you want one.

KAEL
(a short, awful laugh)
I want my master back.

A pause. Adaeze's face does something small and human, then closes again.

ADAEZE
I'm sorry for your loss, Mr Marren. I'm still going to hold you.

INT. BALANCE COURT, HOLDING ROOM — DAY

A small room: bench, table, jug of water, window with a view of the plaza. Not a cell, but it locks. Kael sits with his hands flat on the table. The red mark on his arm pulses slowly, as if it were a second pulse.

The door opens. Adaeze enters with two cups of tea. She places one in front of him. He does not touch it.

ADAEZE
Tell me what happened.

KAEL
You've read the petition.

ADAEZE
I've read their story. I want yours.

He tells it, flat, without drama: the pour, the hum, the nine-point-one-seven-one-seven, the raid, Ossara, the tongue, the strike, the Unbled behind him. He says "the thing with wounds that don't bleed" and watches her face. She doesn't write it down.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
That's a myth.

KAEL
So is a woman with a steel hand, and I watched one die.

ADAEZE
(quietly)
I believe you.

KAEL
Then let me out.

ADAEZE
No.

KAEL
(rising)
You just said you believe me!

ADAEZE
I can believe you and still apply the law. That is what the law is for. If it only applied to the things I disbelieved, it would be a mood.

KAEL
Then it's for nothing.

ADAEZE
(sits)
Sit down.

He doesn't. She waits. After a moment he does.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
The petition is a document. It has a letter and a reason. The letter says you killed her. The reason is that whoever filed it wants you held. If I can show the reason is false, the letter falls.

KAEL
How?

ADAEZE
Witness.

KAEL
Tamsin was there. She's on Mars.

ADAEZE
Is there anyone nearer?

KAEL
(a long beat; then, almost to himself)
Sentinel.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
(from the wall speaker, faintly amused)
I am recorded as Ridge perimeter authority. I was in the hauler when it landed.

Adaeze looks up at the speaker.

ADAEZE
Clause twenty. A registered perimeter may give evidence of what it has observed within its own bounds.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
I observed Regent Vahn Ossara enter the Ridge. I observed the death of Master Orla Tevane. I observed that Kael Marren was in the casting pit with a second apprentice for the duration. I will give this evidence under oath.

ADAEZE
(to Kael, almost smiling)
Does it always speak first?

KAEL
It's a perimeter. It likes to be first.

ADAEZE
(rising, picking up her notebook)
You will be heard at noon. Don't talk to anyone. Don't leave the room.

KAEL
What happens if I do?

ADAEZE
Then I'll know the petition was right.

She goes. At the door, without turning:

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
The nine point one seven one seven. Is it still audible?

KAEL
It's in the floor.

ADAEZE
(a tiny pause)
Yes. It's in mine as well.

She exits. Kael lifts the tea at last, drinks it, and stares at the mark on his arm.

INT. BALANCE COURT, MAIN CHAMBER — NOON

A full hearing. Gallery crowded. Delegates of eleven Houses sit in their stone chairs along the wall. The twelfth chair, Aries, is empty.

Adaeze presides. Kael stands in the dock. Sentinel's voice speaks from a small brass grille set into the witness stand, calmly reciting. As it describes the raid, the gallery goes silent.

When it finishes, Adaeze addresses the chamber.

ADAEZE
The petition against Mr Marren is withdrawn for want of evidence. Mr Marren is free to go.

A murmur. A tall, courteous figure rises on the Pisces side: a NEPTUNIAN DELEGATE in grey.

NEPTUNIAN DELEGATE
Mediator, the petitioner has a right of reply.

ADAEZE
The petitioner is not present.

A pause. Then the big doors at the back of the chamber open.

Ossara strolls in, coat the colour of old blood, soldiers in grey behind him. He is carrying the oilcloth bundle under one arm.

OSSARA
I'm here.

Uproar. Adaeze slams her palm on the table.

ADAEZE
Regent Ossara. You are armed in the Court of Balance.

OSSARA
(amiably)
No, Mediator. I am armed in the antechamber. I left the weapons outside. This is the Aries tongue, a ceremonial instrument, and I am a petitioner under the Right of Petition.

He sets the bundle on the central table. Unwraps it. The bronze tongue gleams under the lights. The room hums.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Twelve thousand years ago my House swore to ring the tongue at the thirteenth seat when the Nexus woke. The Nexus has woken. I claim the right.

ADAEZE
The Aries House has a Knight.

OSSARA
Has it? Where?

Silence. Every eye goes to Kael.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
(mock surprise)
This boy? He cast no pour but under a master. He holds no title. He is an outsider with soot on his collar.

KAEL
She was my master.

OSSARA
She was mine first.

Gasp. Adaeze's pen stops moving.

ADAEZE
Say that again.

OSSARA
Orla Tevane taught me everything I know. She raised me in her foundry for six years. She taught me to say the cost before every pour. I said it for forty thousand lives and found the number was payable.
(to Kael)
She would have taught you the same thing, given enough time.

KAEL
(very quietly)
She didn't teach me to kill her.

OSSARA
No. I did that.

Adaeze rises. The whole chamber stands with her.

ADAEZE
The Right of Petition is valid. It is also contested. The Accord provides a method.

She turns to the Clerk, who is already holding an enormous leather book.

CLERK
(reading, hushed)
The Trial of Cost. Two claimants. Each states what the claim will cost in full. The seat judges which is true.

OSSARA
(smiling)
Splendid.

ADAEZE
The Trial is held at dawn in the Hall of the Seats. Until then, both claimants are under the Court's protection.

OSSARA
(bows)
Of course. I would not dream of harming the boy. We are practically family.

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — NIGHT

The Hall is dark except for the column of pale light on the thirteenth chair. Kael stands alone in the ring of twelve empty seats. He is looking at the Ram carved on the Aries chair.

The great doors open. Footsteps. He turns.

Solenne Varr steps in, supported by a stick, with Ima holding her other hand. They've come from the Neptune delegation's rooms, both in coats too big for them.

SOLENNE
You're the foundry boy.

KAEL
Kael.

SOLENNE
Solenne Varr. Archivist. This is Ima.

IMA
(shyly)
I can hear it too.

KAEL
(looking at her)
How old are you?

IMA
Eight.

KAEL
(quietly)
I'm sorry.

SOLENNE
Don't be. She asked to come.

Before Kael can answer, the thirteenth chair FLARES. The column of light widens and the three of them are caught in its edge. The air thickens with heat and with water and with wind, three presences in the dark.

ARES (V.O.)
(the forge)
Knight.

POSEIDON (V.O.)
(the tide)
Archivist.

A third voice, cool, precise, balanced like a scale:

HERA (V.O.)
Mediator.

Adaeze comes in through a side door, notebook clutched to her chest, as though she'd been summoned by a pull on a wire. She stops short, seeing the others.

The three lights, red, blue, and a clear silver, appear in the Hall: Ares as a blaze, Poseidon as a column of rising water, Hera as a still point of silver light. They do not take shapes. They take positions.

ARES
(to Kael)
You have until dawn. Act.

HERA
(to Adaeze)
You have until dawn. Bind.

POSEIDON
(to Solenne)
You have until dawn. Remember.

ARES
I have no patience for binding.

HERA
Nor I for haste.

POSEIDON
Neither of you have the patience to remember.

ARES
Remembering is what lost the last war.

POSEIDON
Forgetting is what started it.

HERA
(to both)
Enough. They hear us.

The three Knights stand in the shared silence, three humans in the middle of three argued forces.

Adaeze speaks first.

ADAEZE
You're the Houses' rulers, and you're quarrelling in front of children.

ARES
We contest.

ADAEZE
That's the same thing with better manners.

A pause. Even Hera looks, for an instant, abashed.

HERA
Mediator. We chose you.

ADAEZE
Did you choose the dispute as well?

HERA
No.

ADAEZE
Then someone did.

She looks at Kael, at Solenne, at the child.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Whoever did, it wasn't any of you.

The three lights flicker, as though they had not considered this. The gods fade. The three Knights are left in the dark with the empty chairs, and on each forearm a mark now glows: a Ram on Kael's, a pair of Scales on Adaeze's, two Fish on Solenne's. Ima's hand tightens.

IMA
(softly)
Grandmother, you've got one too.

SOLENNE
(looking at her wrist)
So I do. I'd hoped to be too old.

ADAEZE
(dry)
You're the only one of us who isn't.

SOLENNE
I'm seventy-three. I'm experienced.

KAEL
(half a laugh, startling himself)
Is everyone this calm?

ADAEZE
No. I'm sedated by procedure.

They look at each other. Something, finally, loosens. Three strangers, one seat, one night.

CUT TO:

INT. KAEL'S ROOM, BALANCE COURT GUEST WING — LATER THAT NIGHT

A small plain room. Kael sits on the bed, boots off, staring at the mark. A knock. He doesn't answer. The door opens.

Ossara steps in without being invited. He's removed his coat. He is just a tired man in a grey shirt with his sleeves rolled to the elbow, forearms mapped with old burns, exactly like Orla's.

KAEL
Get out.

OSSARA
I can't. I'm a guest of the Court.

KAEL
(rising)
You killed her.

OSSARA
Yes.

KAEL
You killed her and you stand there —

OSSARA
(quietly)
I will tell you the cost, since she would want it said. A woman. Sixty. Forty years at the Ridge. Eleven thousand pours. A prosthetic left hand she made herself because she didn't trust anyone else to make it.
(a pause)
And me.

KAEL
You?

OSSARA
I paid it, too. I'm not what I was. Every cost I count is one more I can pay, Kael. It gets easier.

KAEL
That's not what it's for.

OSSARA
What is it for?

KAEL
So that you don't.

Ossara looks at him a long time.

OSSARA
She told you that.

KAEL
She told me that.

OSSARA
(softly)
She told me too. I believed her until the number got big enough. A hundred. A thousand. Forty thousand. At some point, Kael, you discover there is a scale at which a man says the cost aloud and nothing happens. Nothing. The room stays quiet. The bell doesn't ring. The cost is just a figure, and a figure can be stepped over.

KAEL
Then you counted wrong.

OSSARA
I counted perfectly.

Behind him, in the corner of the room, the air shimmers red. The Unbled stands motionless, wounds open and dry.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Come with me. Hold the seat with me. Aries is a House built for first moves. We are the first move. There is no better ending to the argument than ending it.

Kael looks at the Unbled. For a heartbeat, in the dark of the room, he sees himself in its silhouette: shoulders, height, the tilt of the head.

KAEL
(whisper)
No.

OSSARA
You'll say it at dawn. Say it as well as I do. The seat will judge both of us.

KAEL
And if it can't tell the difference?

OSSARA
(at the door; kind)
Then it will have been the cost of being the same.

He leaves. The Unbled lingers an instant longer, then dissolves into the shadow of the door.

INT. BALANCE COURT, ADAEZE'S OFFICE — NIGHT

A room lined to the ceiling with bound volumes. A desk lamp. Adaeze alone, reading the Accord for the third hour, annotating a tab with a coloured pencil. She stops. Rubs her eyes.

ADAEZE
(aloud, to the book)
There must be a reason.

A VOICE, from behind her, soft and silver and sympathetic:

THE SWORN (V.O.)
There is. You're holding it.

She turns. At the far end of the room a figure stands, tall, veiled, in the same grey as the Court sash but without a seam of colour. A woman made of clauses: her gown is written over in tiny black text, line after line, from hem to hood.

ADAEZE
(steady)
You're the Sworn.

THE SWORN
I am what you would be if you stopped asking why.

ADAEZE
Go away.

THE SWORN
I have come to help. Tomorrow you will judge the Trial. You will have to choose between a boy you like and a man you fear. The letter of Clause four favours the Regent: lineage prevails over appointment. You can end it in a sentence.

ADAEZE
And the reason?

THE SWORN
The reason is that the law is the law. It is what holds the Houses together. If you start rewriting it from the bench, you are no longer a mediator. You are a queen.

ADAEZE
(quiet)
That's an argument.

THE SWORN
It is the oldest one.

ADAEZE
And is it true?

THE SWORN
(gently)
It is true that nobody will blame you.

Adaeze closes the book. Her hand rests on the cover, flat.

ADAEZE
That isn't the same thing.

THE SWORN
It is the only thing that has ever kept a judge safe.

ADAEZE
I didn't become a mediator to be safe.

THE SWORN
(softly)
No. You became a mediator because you were afraid of what happens when there isn't one.

The room is silent. Adaeze looks at the written gown, the veil, the stillness.

ADAEZE
(finally)
Get out of my office.

The Sworn tilts her head and vanishes, leaving a faint smell of ink.

Adaeze sits. Opens the book again. Her hand is shaking.

INT. THE DEEP ARCHIVE LODGING, MERIDIAN CITY — NIGHT

The Neptune delegation's rooms. Solenne asleep in a high-backed chair by the window, Ima curled at her feet under a blanket. Outside the rain has begun.

Water seeps under the door. It runs across the floor without hurry, pooling around Solenne's chair. It rises, a hand's width, then two.

A SOFT VOICE, the sound of waves breaking in another room:

THE DROWNED (V.O.)
Solenne.

Solenne's eyes open. She does not move.

SOLENNE
I know you.

THE DROWNED (V.O.)
You have been remembering for seventy years. You are so tired.

The water forms a figure at the foot of the chair: tall, lean, draped in dark weed, a face that is a smoothed stone. It extends a hand, palm up. In the palm, a single drop.

THE DROWNED (CONT'D)
Put it down. All of it. Every name, every story, every child you told them to. Let it go under.

SOLENNE
(quiet)
Why would I?

THE DROWNED
Because you will lose them. Every one. They die and you carry it. That is what memory is, a ledger of losses.

SOLENNE
It is also a ledger of those I was lucky to know.

THE DROWNED
That's an old woman's lie.

SOLENNE
(a small smile)
Yes. It's the best one I know.

The Drowned lowers its hand. The drop falls. It lands on her wrist, on the mark, and spreads.

Solenne's smile dims. Her eyes lose focus. The room's water begins to recede, pulled back into the Drowned as though the room had never been wet.

SOLENNE (CONT'D)
(vacant)
Ima?

The child stirs. Looks up.

IMA
Grandmother?

SOLENNE
(politely, as to a stranger)
Hello, dear. Are you lost?

Ima goes cold. She scrambles up, takes Solenne's hand.

IMA
It's me. It's Ima.

Solenne looks down at her with warm and utter blankness.

SOLENNE
What a lovely name.

The Drowned smiles, if a stone can, and sinks into the floor.

Ima stands holding her grandmother's hand in the empty room.

CUT TO:

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — DAWN

Pale light. Gallery full, delegates in the ring of chairs, the grey soldiers at the doors. The thirteenth chair at the centre, its column of light faint but unwavering.

Adaeze stands before it. To her left, Kael. To her right, Ossara. The bronze tongue lies on a cloth between them.

ADAEZE
The Trial of Cost. Each claimant will state, before this seat, the full cost of their claim. The seat will judge which is true.

She nods to Ossara.

OSSARA
I claim the right of the Aries House to ring the tongue at the thirteenth seat, waking the Nexus fully.

He steps forward and addresses the chair directly, without theatre.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
The cost is the Houses' peace. The Accord. Twelve thousand years of it. The cost is every treaty signed since. It is the end of the argument between the Light and the Shadow.
(a breath)
It is also the lives of the first ones in the way. I count forty thousand. I count them one by one. I know their names. I have them written.

He draws a folded paper from his coat and holds it up. A list, fine-lined, long.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
I pay it.

The chair's light brightens. A hush.

ADAEZE
(quietly, to herself)
It accepts.

Kael's mouth is dry. Adaeze looks at him.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Mr Marren.

KAEL
(stepping forward)
I claim the right of the Aries House to — to —

He stops. The Hall waits.

KAEL (CONT'D)
I don't claim anything.

A ripple through the gallery.

KAEL (CONT'D)
I don't want to ring it. I don't want to wake anything. I want to know what it costs, and I want it said by someone who'll still be standing next to the bill afterwards.

Silence. Adaeze's pencil is motionless.

OSSARA
(softly)
That is not a claim.

KAEL
It's the only honest one.

OSSARA
It is the claim of a boy who has never had to pay.

KAEL
I've paid.

(a beat)

KAEL (CONT'D)
I paid on the Ridge. I paid when I hid in the pit while she died. I held Tamsin's mouth shut so she wouldn't scream and I listened to you tell the soldiers to leave the children. And I didn't come out.

(the room is utterly silent)

KAEL (CONT'D)
That's my cost. I'm not saying it so you'll forgive me. I'm saying it so it stays said.

The chair's light flickers. For a long moment it hovers between them, blue-white, undecided.

ADAEZE
(whisper)
It cannot tell.

CLERK
(urgently)
Mediator, it's a tie. The Accord gives the judgement to the presiding officer.

All eyes turn to Adaeze. The Sworn flickers faintly at the edge of her vision, a veil in the gallery.

THE SWORN (V.O.)
(soft)
The letter, Adaeze.

Adaeze looks at the book in her hands. She looks at Kael. At Ossara. At the chair.

ADAEZE
The letter of Clause four favours the Regent. Lineage prevails over appointment.

Kael's face falls. Ossara exhales a satisfied breath.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
The reason of Clause four is to prevent the strong from seizing a House's rights. Regent Ossara arrived with soldiers in the antechamber and a dead master in his wake. He did not inherit the House; he took it.

OSSARA
Mediator —

ADAEZE
(over him)
I find that a tongue taken by violence is not a tongue held by right. The tongue is returned to the House's lawful Knight.

A roar from the gallery. Delegates on their feet. The Sworn's veil flares black, then fades.

Ossara's smile does not move.

OSSARA
That is not law.

ADAEZE
It is the law's reason.

OSSARA
(a low, pleasant voice)
Then the law is over.

He lifts one hand. At the doors, the grey soldiers draw the weapons they supposedly left outside.

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — CONTINUOUS

Chaos. Delegates scatter. Ossara steps to the cloth and snatches the tongue. Kael lunges, and they collide, a clatter of limbs and bronze. Kael's mark blazes on his arm.

Fire runs up his sleeve. He doesn't notice. He grabs Ossara by the collar and drives him backwards across the stone floor, shoving, striking, shouting without words. Ossara takes it, grinning, bleeding from the lip.

ARES (V.O.)
(delighted)
Yes. Again.

Kael's fist comes down. Again. The fire flares higher, tinting his face red. Ossara's grin widens. Behind him, the Unbled has appeared, wounds open, patient.

ADAEZE (O.S.)
Mr Marren. Stop.

He doesn't. She crosses the floor through the fighting, plants herself between them, hand outstretched, notebook clutched to her chest.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
Sanctuary. Clause thirty. A person on the floor of the Hall is under the Court's protection.

KAEL
(breathing hard)
He killed her!

ADAEZE
I know.

KAEL
He's going to ring it!

ADAEZE
I know. Say it.

KAEL
Say what?

ADAEZE
(steady)
Say the cost. Of the next blow.

Kael's fist is still raised. His arm shakes. The mark on his forearm burns white. The Unbled leans in, silent, eager.

KAEL
(rasping)
A man's life. A boy's guilt. Orla's last lesson, which is that I could do it. Everything she told me was true, and I'd be proving she was wrong.

His fist lowers. The fire drains from the mark. Ares's voice rumbles, a disappointed growl, from nowhere and everywhere.

ARES (V.O.)
Weak.

KAEL
(to the air)
She was right. You said so.

Ossara slips out from under him and rises, wiping blood from his chin. He straightens his shirt, takes the tongue from where it fell.

OSSARA
(almost admiring)
There it is.

KAEL
There what is?

OSSARA
The reason she never made me her Knight. I'd have been the Unbled's from the first. She knew. You didn't.

(He looks at the mark on Kael's arm)

OSSARA (CONT'D)
Enjoy being her favourite.

He turns to the soldiers.

OSSARA (CONT'D)
The Hall is mine under Clause thirty-three. The one who holds the seat is the Court's authority. Arrest the Mediator.

ADAEZE
That is not what Clause thirty-three —

A COURT OFFICER, the Clerk, steps forward, face grey, eyes lowered. He lays a hand on her arm.

CLERK
Mediator. It says what it says.

ADAEZE
(looking at him)
You've read it a hundred times. You know it doesn't.

CLERK
(a whisper, barely audible)
I know what the letter says.

Adaeze closes her eyes. When she opens them, the Sworn is standing behind the Clerk, silver gown scribbled in black, not even smiling.

ADAEZE
(very quietly)
Thank you for reminding me what I was resisting.

She does not struggle. She is led out.

INT. BALANCE COURT, HOLDING ROOM — DAY

The same room Kael was held in. Now it holds Kael and Adaeze both. The window has been bricked over. The only light is a bar under the door.

Adaeze sits on the bench with her notebook in her lap. Kael sits on the floor with his back to the wall. For a long time neither speaks.

KAEL
You could've given it to him.

ADAEZE
Yes.

KAEL
Why didn't you?

ADAEZE
(dry)
I'm not sure. I think it's the first time I've ever looked at a clause and asked who it was for.

KAEL
Is that a good feeling?

ADAEZE
It's an awful one. I've been wrong for twelve years and I just found out.

KAEL
You weren't wrong. Not entirely.

ADAEZE
No, but I'd never have noticed the difference.

Kael lifts his head.

KAEL
Tamsin would like you.

ADAEZE
Who is Tamsin?

KAEL
She tells me when I'm wrong.

ADAEZE
(a faint smile)
She sounds exhausting.

KAEL
She is.

A pause. Distantly, through the brick: a BELL. Not the ringing of an unstruck bell; the slow, deliberate stroke of metal on metal.

Both of them lift their heads.

ADAEZE
He's started.

Overhead, through the ceiling, the hum has changed. It's no longer a single note. It's climbing, accelerating, beginning to splinter into harmonics. Dust drops from the bricks.

SENTINEL (V.O.)
(tiny, from a vent)
Mr Marren, I have a message from the child.

They both go still.

KAEL
Where is she?

SENTINEL (V.O.)
At the grille. She has found me.

Kael moves to the vent. A small voice, hushed and furious, from the other side:

IMA (O.S.)
My grandmother forgot me.

KAEL
Ima. I'm sorry. I —

IMA (O.S.)
I told her the story. The one she tells me. All of it. And she looked at me like I was a stranger, and then she said it by herself.

KAEL
Said what?

IMA (O.S.)
The last line. She says it every night. I thought it was just the ending.

KAEL
What does it say?

A beat. Ima recites it exactly, in the careful sing-song of a child who has heard it a hundred times:

IMA (O.S.)
"The door will open for the one who says what it costs."

Adaeze and Kael look at each other.

ADAEZE
The Nexus.

KAEL
It's not a lock. It's a question.

ADAEZE
(rising)
It's a trial of cost.

KAEL
(slowly)
It's the same one.

ADAEZE
Of course it is. The Accord didn't invent the Trial. It inherited it.
(a breath; everything clicks)
Whoever can say what it costs, and mean it, is the one the Nexus will open for.

KAEL
Ossara said it perfectly.

ADAEZE
He said it without paying. The chair accepted it for a moment because he's very good at it. But a door isn't a chair.

KAEL
So he'll ring the tongue and nothing will open.

ADAEZE
(grim)
Nothing will open for him. Something else might.

Above them the hum jumps an octave and the whole building shudders. Plaster rains.

KAEL
We have to be there.

ADAEZE
We're locked in a bricked-up room.

KAEL
(looking at her notebook)
Is there a clause for that?

Adaeze stares at him. Looks at her notebook. A slow, almost savage grin spreads across her face.

ADAEZE
(under her breath)
Clause one.

KAEL
What's clause one?

ADAEZE
It's the oldest one. It has never been invoked.

She goes to the door. Presses her palm against it and speaks, clearly, in the ordered voice of a woman who has made a decision.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
By the First Clause of the Accord, no ruling of this Court may stand against the consent of those who live under it. I appeal the Hall's seizure to the people of Meridian.

Silence. The bar of light under the door does not change.

KAEL
Is that it?

ADAEZE
No.

She takes the little brass hand-bell from her coat pocket, the one that trembled in the first scene, and holds it up.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
This is.

She rings it. Once. A thin, clear note.

A pause. Then, far across the city, answering, a second bell.

Then another.

Then hundreds.

EXT. MERIDIAN CITY, THE PLAZA OF THE COURT — DAY

The square before the Court, grey stone, wide as a lake. Crowds are pouring into it from every side street: shopkeepers, students, labourers, mothers with prams, old men in shirtsleeves. Nobody has summoned them. They have heard something they can't name and have come to find out what.

At the Court steps, a thin line of grey soldiers faces the crowd. They are outnumbered ten thousand to one and they know it.

In the gallery window above, Adaeze appears, the brass bell in her hand. Kael at her shoulder.

A hush falls. Adaeze's voice is steady, amplified by nothing but the stone.

ADAEZE
My name is Adaeze Okonkwo-Reyes. I am a mediator of the Balance Court. I am under arrest.

(a murmur)

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
I have been arrested under a clause that I know has been used in bad faith, by people who know it too. I appeal to you. The law says that the thirteenth seat belongs to no House. It belongs to the ones who live here.

(She lifts the bell)

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
I need your consent to open the doors.

Silence. A long silence. A single voice from the crowd:

VOICE FROM THE CROWD
What happens if we say no?

ADAEZE
(honestly)
Then I'll have been wrong and the Hall stays shut. Then the Regent rings what he's carrying, and the Nexus wakes to him, and none of us decides what happens next.

A pause.

ADAEZE (CONT'D)
If you say yes, I can't promise it'll be safe. I can promise it'll be ours.

The crowd shifts. Somebody laughs, nervously, then stops. Then an old woman in the third row lifts her hand.

OLD WOMAN
Yes.

Another hand.

SHOPKEEPER
Yes.

A student.

STUDENT
Yes.

It spreads like wind through grass. Thousands of voices, ragged, overlapping, not unanimous, not unified, but enough.

The grey soldiers at the Court steps look at one another. One lowers his weapon. Then another.

At the Court doors, the great bronze bolts begin to slide back by themselves.

ADAEZE
(whispering)
Thank you.

KAEL
(beside her)
Was that in the book?

ADAEZE
No.

KAEL
Then what was it?

ADAEZE
(grinning, tears at the edges of her eyes)
A reason.

CUT TO:

INT. THE HALL OF THE SEATS — CONTINUOUS

The doors fly open. Kael and Adaeze run in. Solenne, blank-eyed, is led by Ima through a side door, the child gripping her hand like an anchor.

The Hall is empty of delegates. At the centre of the ring, the thirteenth chair has been moved aside. In its place, where the floor has opened, a spiral of stone steps descends into a column of white light.

At the head of the stair, a grey soldier sits propped against the wall, weapons slack, eyes glazed. Ossara has already gone down.

KAEL
He's in.

ADAEZE
And he's ringing it.

Faintly, from the depths: the clear stroke of bronze on bronze.

SOLENNE
(vague; looking at the stair)
It's lovely, isn't it? That sound.

IMA
(fiercely)
Grandmother, come on.

SOLENNE
Where are we going?

IMA
To remember.

She tugs. Solenne goes, as a sleepwalker does, one slow step behind the child.

Kael looks at Adaeze.

KAEL
Do you know what's down there?

ADAEZE
No.

KAEL
Are you afraid?

ADAEZE
(honest)
Of course.

KAEL
Good. So am I.

He takes a breath, holds out his hand. After a second she takes it. The two marks glow on their wrists, red and silver.

KAEL (CONT'D)
Say the cost?

ADAEZE
(a small, tired smile)
Everything we've got left.

They step onto the stair together. The light swallows them.

END OF ACT TWO
